# Dataverse Contact Provider

The Dataverse Contact API sits in front of Microsoft Dataverse and only exposes
the tables, fields and custom actions that have been explicitly configured for
it. This provider manages that configuration: which Dataverse tables are
published to the API, which fields callers can read and write, which Dataverse
Custom APIs are callable, and the baseline permissions every authenticated
caller gets in each scope.

With it, a whole portal backend (e.g. a citizen booking service) can be
provisioned with `terraform apply`, reviewed in version control, and reproduced
in a new environment in minutes.

New here? Start with the [Getting started](guides/getting-started) guide. The
[Permissions](guides/permissions) and
[Parent-scoped children](guides/parent-scoped-children) guides cover the two
concepts that most often trip people up. [Custom APIs](guides/custom-apis)
covers who may call a Dataverse function or action, and on which record.

## Example Usage

{{ tffile "examples/provider/provider.tf" }}

Authentication is the pre-shared admin connection key, sent as the admin Bearer
token. It must be byte-identical to `ADMIN_CONNECTION_KEY` on the API
deployment. Prefer supplying it via the `DATAVERSE_CONTACT_CONNECTION_KEY`
environment variable rather than in configuration.

Each request gets `timeout` (default 60 seconds) per attempt and is retried up
to `max_retries` times (default 3) with a growing wait when the API doesn't
answer, is rate limited, or answers 502, 503 or 504, as a cold or busy
deployment can. Publishing, unpublishing and removing are retried only on 429
(rate limited): after a timeout or a gateway error the API may already have
acted, and a second publish would then fail. If a pipeline still times out
against a slow API, raise `timeout` (or set `DATAVERSE_CONTACT_TIMEOUT`, e.g.
`2m`); a lower `terraform apply -parallelism` also takes load off the API.

~> Every scope needs exactly one `dataversecontact_permissions_sync` resource.
A scope with no published defaults grants nothing — every route answers
`403 Missing required permission`, including reads.

